    "Morning activity": {
      "es": [
        [
          "Hyperarch cervical rotation",
          "2'",
          [],
          "Purpose:\nTo train controlled cervical rotation, upper-neck proprioception, and the muscles and fascia structures associated with the upper cervical region, primarily around C1 to C4.\n\nStarting position:\nSit or stand tall with your head centered, chin level, shoulders relaxed, and chest facing forward.\n\nInstructions:\n\n* Gently lengthen the back of your neck without forcing your chin downward.\n\n* Slowly rotate your head to one side while keeping your chin level.\n\n* Stop at a comfortable range before pain, dizziness, or compensation begins.\n\n* Slowly return your head to the center.\n\nRepeat on the opposite side.\n\nKeep your feet firmly pressed down and grip the ground.\n\nPrimary cue:\n* Keep the movement smooth and controlled.\n\nImagine rotating around a tall vertical axis.\n\nKeep both shoulders facing forward.\n\nDo not tilt, extend, or force the neck.\n\nFeel gentle activation at the base of the skull and along the side of the neck.\n\nMaintain relaxed breathing.\nUse only a pain-free range.\n\nDosage:\nPerform 1 - 2 min duration.\n\nImportant note:\nThis movement may engage muscles and sensory pathways associated with the upper cervical region.\n\nStop immediately if you experience:\nRadiating arm pain, numbness, tingling, weakness, significant dizziness, nausea, severe headache, or loss of balance."
